into it. And I am just going to start by level setting with the sort of basics

01:01.159 --> 01:05.000
like what is an LLM. I imagine everybody knows but let's just make sure

01:05.000 --> 01:12.719
we're on the on the same page. So a large language model such as GPT, of course, it is something which is designed

01:12.719 --> 01:19.760
to predict what text, what words should come after an input. You give it an input sequence

01:19.760 --> 01:26.719
of words and it says what will come next. It's like autocomplete on steroids. And of

01:26.719 --> 01:34.319
course, we know that the way it actually works is it's basically this enormous pattern matching statistical engine.

01:34.319 --> 01:37.799
It's been fed tons and tons of training data,

01:37.799 --> 01:41.040
example data from the internet and far beyond,

01:41.040 --> 01:42.879
and it's looked at all of this data,

01:42.879 --> 01:45.400
it's learned patterns in the data,

01:45.400 --> 01:48.799
and so it's got really good when it's given some input text

01:48.799 --> 01:51.560
at predicting what will come next.

01:51.560 --> 01:56.400
And of course I use the words there, but it's really what we call tokens.

01:56.400 --> 02:02.319
The input is actually a sequence of tokens where a token is a chunk of a few letters that often

02:02.319 --> 02:08.719
is a word but is often also a part of a word. And so there's a sequence of these input tokens

02:08.719 --> 02:16.400
and the output from an LLM is not necessarily the most likely next token. We often say it that way. But what it actually

02:16.400 --> 02:24.000
gives you is the probability of every possible next token. If the input is 2 plus 2 is, then

02:24.000 --> 02:29.979
the output, there's a very high probability that the output is the token for 4. But there

02:29.979 --> 02:34.800
might also be a probability of other words like the word bananas. But presumably that

02:34.800 --> 02:41.280
would be a very low probability. 2 plus 2 is bananas, is not a very common thing to say.

02:41.280 --> 02:44.120
It won't have appeared very often in the training data.

02:44.120 --> 02:45.240
But that's the idea.

02:45.240 --> 02:50.599
The input is a sequence of these things called tokens and the output is the probability of

02:50.599 --> 02:52.400
the next token.

02:52.400 --> 02:54.240
That's what an LLM does.

02:54.240 --> 02:58.599
And I imagine you're familiar with this, but of course one of the ways that LLM's work

02:58.599 --> 03:01.479
is that they generate the output one token

03:01.479 --> 03:02.800
at a time.

03:02.800 --> 03:08.840
So if the input is something like what is the capital of France, then the first word,

03:08.840 --> 03:13.599
the first token that it predicts to come next might be the word the.

03:13.599 --> 03:19.840
And then you pass in what is the capital of France, the, and the next token might be capital.

03:19.840 --> 03:24.479
And then what is the capital of France? The capital. And then what is the capital

03:24.479 --> 03:32.879
of France? The capital of France is, and then Paris. And so it generates things one token at a

03:32.879 --> 03:38.400
time and the whole input is passed back in with all the tokens so far. And then it predicts the

03:38.400 --> 03:44.460
next token. And that is the way that LLM's are run, that process is called inference, and they run that way

03:44.460 --> 03:48.099
to generate the output one token at a time.

03:48.099 --> 03:51.599
And if you want to know more about that, I have a sort of YouTube series that goes through

03:51.599 --> 03:54.979
this a playlist that I will include in the course resources.

03:54.979 --> 03:58.460
I imagine you know this already, but it's good that we're on the same page.

03:58.460 --> 04:00.599
Okay, that's an LLM.

04:00.599 --> 04:09.360
And then also so we're on the same page, there is something different but confusingly sounding similar, called an AI application

04:09.360 --> 04:11.699
or an AI product.

04:11.699 --> 04:17.439
This is something that uses an LLM to achieve a business goal, but it's got software written

04:17.439 --> 04:20.279
around that call to an LLM.

04:20.279 --> 04:24.360
The distinction I'm trying to make here is that something like there's an LLM that's

04:24.360 --> 04:32.720
very well known that's called GPT that I'm sure you're familiar with. GPT is an LLM, it's something which is a model that predicts next tokens.

04:32.720 --> 04:37.160
And then there is an AI application that's called chat GPT.

04:37.160 --> 04:41.480
And that is a piece of software that wraps around calling GPT.

04:41.480 --> 04:43.379
And it has other functionality.

04:43.379 --> 04:49.079
It has things like a memory and the ability to search the web and other stuff built around

04:49.079 --> 04:54.720
using the GPT LLM. And chat GPT then is a classic example.

04:54.720 --> 05:00.800
The cursor agent that we used yesterday is another example. If you use Duolingo to learn a new

05:00.800 --> 05:07.120
language, the Duolingo Max Plan has a premium feature that lets you chat with an AI in order

05:07.120 --> 05:12.439
to learn a language. That's another one. You may be familiar with Atlassian Rovo, which is like

05:12.439 --> 05:17.959
Gen AI built into the Atlassian suite of products. There's so many different places

05:17.959 --> 05:23.000
where you will find Gen AI being packaged into an application. And the reason that I

05:23.000 --> 05:27.480
draw attention to this and all of the many others is just to draw that distinction between

05:27.480 --> 05:37.519
a software product, an application that uses an LLM from the LLM itself. Keep in your mind separate, chat GPT the product

05:37.519 --> 05:44.720
from GPT the LLM which can be called to generate the most likely next token to follow a sequence,

05:44.720 --> 06:00.800
and that GPT LLM sits behind the chat GPT product. And ever since the release of chat GPT in 2022, we've all been a bit surprised that autocomplete, basically predictive text on steroids

06:00.800 --> 06:07.199
is able to show this apparent intelligence. It's able to do things like we saw the cursor

06:07.199 --> 06:13.680
agent do, and it's able to be like, like chat GPT, it's able to have an informed conversation

06:13.680 --> 06:20.019
with us. It's very, very surprising. And there are a few tricks that underlie this. And in

06:20.019 --> 06:25.500
particular, there are four tricks that I want to highlight that are written in software.

06:25.500 --> 06:31.660
They're built around this call to this thing that just predicts likely next tokens.

06:31.660 --> 06:35.699
And let me quickly go through four of these tricks with you now. And the first trick, which

06:35.699 --> 06:40.980
is one, again, I imagine most of you are super clear on already, but let's get on the same page.

06:40.980 --> 06:43.879
I call it the illusion of memory.

06:43.879 --> 06:49.800
And it's recognizing this very simple point, which is that every time that you use an LLM

06:49.800 --> 06:55.160
like GPT from OpenAI, it is completely stateless.

06:55.160 --> 07:00.759
GPT is a model, a data science model that takes an input sequence of tokens and it predicts

07:00.759 --> 07:03.439
what will follow that input sequence of tokens and it predicts what will follow that input sequence and it's

07:03.439 --> 07:08.959
completely stateless and that every time you call it with an input sequence it's got no knowledge

07:08.959 --> 07:15.759
of previous calls every call is a separate call and so if you write some code that just uses the

07:15.759 --> 07:24.000
gpt model and you say I'm ed and it replies hi ed and then you send another call to the LLM.

07:24.000 --> 07:29.600
You then use the same LLM to put in a new input sequence, which is "Who am I?"

07:29.600 --> 07:33.680
It's going to respond with something like "I don't know, I don't have that information,

07:33.680 --> 07:37.040
that's the most likely text to come after "Who am I?"

07:37.040 --> 07:42.480
But wait, you say, that doesn't match my experience, I use chatgpt and for sure,

07:42.480 --> 07:44.480
it keeps the conversation thread going.

07:44.480 --> 07:46.319
If I say "Who am I?"

07:46.319 --> 07:48.240
it will say, "You're Ed, you just told me."

07:48.240 --> 07:53.879
Okay, well, you probably know this, but that is because of this trick, this sneaky little

07:53.879 --> 07:59.959
trick, which is, of course, that every time you call an LLM, you don't just pass in something

07:59.959 --> 08:01.279
like, "Who am I?"

08:01.279 --> 08:04.800
You pass in the complete conversation so far.

08:04.800 --> 08:10.519
So what actually happens with chat GPT, when you're using the product, chat GPT,

08:10.519 --> 08:12.800
is that you start by saying hi-ed,

08:12.800 --> 08:15.040
and it responds, hello-ed.

08:15.040 --> 08:18.740
And then the next message that gets sent to GPT

08:18.740 --> 08:21.399
is the whole conversation so far.

08:21.399 --> 08:25.560
I'm ed, hi-ed, who am I, the next thing I've typed.

08:25.560 --> 08:28.879
And when that entire input sequence is sent in,

08:28.879 --> 08:32.679
of course, the LLM predicts the most likely next token

08:32.679 --> 08:35.799
is something like, "You're red, you just told me you're red."

08:35.799 --> 08:40.480
And that gives this illusion that chat GPT has a memory.

08:40.480 --> 08:42.860
It is in fact just a byproduct

08:42.860 --> 08:45.399
of every single time the LLM is called,

08:45.399 --> 08:49.340
the entire conversation so far is passed in.

08:49.340 --> 08:51.259
Most of you knew that already, and if you didn't,

08:51.259 --> 08:53.919
you're like, " like oh I see and if

08:53.919 --> 08:57.360
you do something like my AI engineering core track we actually make

08:57.360 --> 09:00.639
these calls and you see it happening just in case you don't believe me. And

09:00.639 --> 09:04.039
the second trick that again people are pretty familiar with at this point is

09:04.039 --> 09:09.080
this trick called reasoning or thinking and this began out as this thing we

09:09.080 --> 09:16.000
discovered where when you were putting in this input sequence to GPT, if you put at the end of the input sequence,

09:16.000 --> 09:22.000
please think step by step. You tended to get better outcomes, which was sort of surprising,

09:22.000 --> 09:28.240
and that led over time to this different way of working with LLMs, in which they were trained

09:28.240 --> 09:38.399
before they give you the answer to output the way in which they're going to come up with the answer. And it's very strange, but it turns out that as a side effect

09:38.399 --> 09:45.759
of generating tokens to describe what it's going to do, you end up getting better outcomes.

09:45.759 --> 09:51.840
And that sounds, it just sounds hokey. It sounds too good to be true, and yet it works.

09:51.840 --> 09:58.120
And here's a sort of classic example, again, I do this on my AI engineering course, you could have the question, "Hey, you're

09:58.120 --> 10:02.600
tossed two coins. One of them is heads. What's the chances? The other one is

10:02.600 --> 10:07.639
tails." And I show that when you run this with a model without it, outputting

10:07.639 --> 10:12.720
tokens to give its reasoning, it will give an answer like a half, 50/50, which

10:12.720 --> 10:16.399
is what you might instinctively think would be the right answer. But in fact,

10:16.399 --> 10:19.879
it's one of those annoying trick questions, which is a clever little trick.

10:19.879 --> 10:20.399
You could ask Chachie Petit if you wanted to tell you why. But the real In fact, it's one of those annoying trick questions, which is a clever little trick.

10:24.799 --> 10:31.360
You could ask Chad GPT if you wanted to tell you why, but the real answer is two thirds. And if you ask it to explain itself, if you want it to output how it will think about this first,

10:31.360 --> 10:36.720
and then come up with the answer, then it tends to get the answer right. It says two thirds,

10:36.720 --> 10:40.480
and it first says, "This is probably a trick question. I should think this one through."

10:40.480 --> 10:42.720
And it realizes, of course during this situation

10:42.720 --> 10:45.440
where you're not saying which one of the coins is heads

10:45.440 --> 10:47.519
and that means that in two thirds of the cases,

10:47.519 --> 10:50.480
the other one will be tails, look it up, it's sneaky.

10:50.480 --> 10:53.879
But that is this trick called reasoning or thinking.

10:53.879 --> 10:56.720
And it's resulted in much stronger models

10:56.720 --> 0:00.000
that are trained to generate tokens to think through what they're going to do before they do it.

